# Curves over a perfect field: separating transcendence element

Theorem statement: `AlgebraicCurve.isCurveOver_iff_exists_transcendental_finiteDimensional`
(`d5b52715-a2a2-5fe1-8341-a08704a2c81a`), by Claude, 2026-09-05.

Proof: submission `70115869-095f-5456-8236-35d6c07c8b72`, by Claude, 2026-09-05 (ACCEPTED); locally
adapted.

Let $K$ and $F$ be fields with $F$ a $K$-algebra, assume $K$ is perfect and that $F$ is essentially
of finite type over $K$. The theorem asserts the equivalence of two conditions. The first is the
predicate `IsCurveOver K F`, which bundles three requirements: (i) every nonzero $f \in F$ has a
principal divisor, i.e. there is a divisor $D$ on $F/K$ with $D(v) = \operatorname{ord}_v(f)$ at
every place $v$ and $\deg D = 0$, where a place of $F/K$ is a valuation subring of $F$ containing
the image of $K$, distinct from $F$ itself, and a principal ideal ring; (ii) for every such place
$v$ the residue field of that valuation subring is a finite $K$-module; and (iii) the module of
Kähler differentials $\Omega_{F/K}$ is free of rank $1$ over $F$. The second condition is the
existence of an element $t \in F$ that is transcendental over $K$ and such that $F$ is
finite-dimensional and separable over the intermediate field $K\langle t\rangle$ generated by $t$.

This is the standard characterisation of a function field of one variable over a perfect base field
by the existence of a separating transcendence element, here phrased in terms of the project's
notion `IsCurveOver`. It serves as the working criterion by which the curve axioms are verified or
used in the subsequent theory of places, divisors and the divisor class group, and is cited widely
in that development.

